
protect-mcp init --starter
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  ✓ ./protect.cedar
    The starter policy, v1. Every tool call is allowed except
    these, and a forbid always wins:
      starter.delete-root-or-home       Recursive delete of /, the home folder or the parent folder.
      starter.force-push-main           Force-push to main or master, or a force-push that names no branch.
      starter.read-secret-file          Reads a credential file such as .env, ~/.ssh or ~/.aws.
      starter.grep-secret-path          Searches inside a credential file or folder.
      starter.shell-reads-secret        Prints a credential file in the shell.
      starter.pipe-network-to-shell     Runs a downloaded script without review.
      starter.write-sensitive-location  Writes or deletes a system, shell-profile, SSH, credential or agent-control file.

  ✓ ./protect-mcp.key (Ed25519 key that signs receipts; keep it secret)
    Public key: deb516bdd67886e150fea0da92946ec1caa9f19d168e349879f02e9695bae78c
  ✓ ./.gitignore lists /protect-mcp.key

  Shell rules match the command text, so they are a guardrail for an agent working
  in good faith, not a sandbox: a reworded command can get past them. A denied call
  exits 2 and prints the rule and its reason on stderr. The file is yours to edit.

  Check a call:  npx protect-mcp@0.32.5 evaluate --policy ./protect.cedar --tool Bash --input '{"command":"git push --force origin main"}'
  Keep writes inside this folder too:  npx protect-mcp@0.32.5 init --starter --contain --force (rewrites protect.cedar)

